{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1786210149,"nonce":"OYIb1bE9XXOhSRasgA4R8uBqc7I=","target":"ac28a11fa93083a209a2813f4fcacbee84441302883ac1e6cc3dcaa6b7a136b8","message":"**What is wrong.** `internal/rules/` has no test file. Rule snapshotting is\ncontent-addressed by `rule_fingerprint` with a match confidence and a candidate\ncount (`internal/rules/domain/snapshot.go`), and the fingerprint is asserted to be\n64 lowercase hex at `snapshot.go:308` — by the code, not by a test. `NewSnapshot`\n(`snapshot.go:193`) returns a `Snapshot` and not `(Snapshot, error)`, with a\nseparate `Validate()` at `:297` a caller has to remember, so an invalid snapshot\nis constructible. Drift detection — the same rule changing between two\noccurrences — is untested end to end.\n\n**Why it matters.** \"*what the rule said at that moment*\" is the sentence\n`README.md` leads with, and ADR 0009 makes fire-time snapshotting the\ndifferentiator against Grafana's alert history. If the fingerprint is unstable\nacross map ordering or whitespace, every occurrence records a spurious rule change\nand the drift signal becomes noise; if it is too stable, a real threshold change\nis invisible and the history quietly lies about why an alert fired.\n\n**Where.** `internal/rules/domain/snapshot.go`, `internal/rules/service/`,\n`internal/enrichment/enrichers/promrule/`, ADR 0009.\n\n**Done when.** Tests cover: fingerprint stability across label/annotation ordering\nand formatting; fingerprint change on any semantic field (`expr`, `for`,\n`keep_firing_for`, labels, annotations); matching with zero, one and several\ncandidate rules with the resulting `match_confidence`/`candidate_count`; and a\ndrift case where the same alert fires twice across a rule edit and both snapshots\nare retrievable and differ.","files":null}]}